<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kłodzko</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Glatz</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kłodzko, in German Glatz, is a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-silesia">Lower Silesia</a>, south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nysa">Nysa</a> Kłodzka at the centre of a basin enclosed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudetes">Sudetes</a>. For centuries it belonged to the Bohemian crown, guarding the road from Silesia into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> annexed it in 1742. Its hilltop fortress was rebuilt under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-the-great">Frederick the Great</a>, and the town passed to Poland in 1945.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/klodzko.jpg" alt="Kłodzko" width="320" height="225" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jacek Halicki · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2024_Szko%C5%82a_Podstawowa_Nr_1_w_K%C5%82odzku_(13).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
